<header class="sp-hero">
    <h1>How to Pay for Treatment in China</h1>
    <p>WeChat Pay, Alipay, cash, and international cards — what actually works at a Chinese hospital, and how billing is structured, so there are no surprises at the cashier.</p>
</header>

<div class="sp-crumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/how-it-works.html">Process</a> › <a href="/payment-guide.html">Payment Guide</a></div>

<main class="sp-shell">
    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill">Most important</span>
        <h2>WeChat Pay &amp; Alipay with a Foreign Card</h2>
        <p>China is largely cashless. The single most useful thing you can do before arriving is <strong>link your international Visa, Mastercard, or JCB card to WeChat Pay and Alipay</strong>. Both apps now support foreign cards and convert charges into your own currency.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Open <strong>WeChat</strong> → <em>Me</em> → <em>Pay</em> (or "Wallet") → <em>Cards</em> → add your international card.</li>
            <li>Open <strong>Alipay</strong> → <em>Account</em> → <em>Bank Cards</em> → add the same or a second card.</li>
            <li>Verify identity with your passport when prompted (a one-time step).</li>
            <li>At the hospital cashier, scan the QR code with either app and pay.</li>
        </ol>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;"><strong>Note:</strong> international cards may carry a small fee (around 3%) for larger transactions. Hospitals are large merchants, so your card is reliably accepted — it is smaller street vendors that sometimes are not.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill">Always works</span>
        <h2>Cash (Chinese Yuan / RMB)</h2>
        <p>Every hospital cashier accepts cash in Chinese yuan. If you prefer cash, plan to <strong>withdraw RMB from a bank ATM</strong> (Bank of China, ICBC, and others accept most foreign cards) or exchange currency at an airport counter or bank branch.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;">For a large surgery deposit, carrying the full amount in cash is impractical and risky — a linked payment app or a bank transfer is better. We can help you arrange a bank transfer for larger payments.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <h2>How Hospital Billing Actually Works</h2>
        <p>Chinese hospitals run on a <strong>pay-as-you-go model</strong>, which is different from the "receive a bill later" model many patients are used to:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Deposit at admission:</strong> you pay an upfront deposit when you register or are admitted.</li>
            <li><strong>Charges accrue:</strong> consultation, tests, procedure, bed, and medication are billed as you go.</li>
            <li><strong>Settlement at discharge:</strong> you pay the balance (or receive a refund) at the end.</li>
            <li><strong>Itemized invoices:</strong> you receive itemized receipts — keep these, they are what your insurer needs for reimbursement.</li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <h2>Payment options compared</h2>
        <table class="visa-table">
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Works at public hospitals?</th><th>Fee</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
